          "text_length": 5574,
          "text_excerpt": "Current Clinical Trials – Flint Animal Cancer Center Skip to content colorado state university CSU flint animal cancer center About Us Our Story FACC Team Contact Us News For Owners Our Services Cancer Information Request Appointments Treatment Options Preparing for Your Visit Frequently Asked Questions For DVMs Consult Line Treatment Options One Cure About One Cure Racing for One Cure Donate to One Cure Clinical Trials About Clinical Trials Current Clinical Trials Education & Research Comparative Oncology Research Oncology Specialty Training Donate Greatest Need Patient Assistance One Cure Scholarship Research Feline Cancer Research FACC Friends menu close Current Clinical Trials Our clinical trials program is one of the largest in veterinary oncology and is supported by seven dedicated staff members. Annually, the program manages an average of 30 clinical trials to study more effective therapies for a variety of cancer types including, osteosarcoma, lymphoma, soft tissue sarcoma, and brain tumors. The following trials are currently enrolling patients. For more information, please contact the clinical trials service at (970) 297-4001 or email [email protected] . In general, to participate in one of our clinical trials, patients must be treated at our facility, but will go home after each appointment, unless the protocol dictates otherwise. Most families who participate in our clinical trials live within 150 miles of Fort Collins or are willing to travel or temporarily relocate for the period of the study. Clinical trials related to pet cancer are conducted throughout the United States. If you do not live close enough to participate in one of our trials, please visit the AVMA Veterinary Clinical Trials Registry website to search for trials in your area. Browse Clinical Trials Search Search clinical trials Cancer Type Filter by cancer type All Cancer Types Bladder tumor (2) Carcinoma (other) (2) Leukemia (1) Lymphoma (1) Osteosarcoma (3) Soft Tissue Sarcoma (1) Species Filter by species All Species Canine (8) Feline (2) Treatment Type Filter by treatment type All Treatment Types Chemotherapy (1) Immunotherapy (3) Other (1) Radiation Therapy (3) Surgery (2) Targeted Therapy (1) Tolerance and Outcomes of Hypofractionated Low-Dose-Rate Abdominal Radiotherapy for Feline Small Cell Gastrointestinal Lymphoma Feline Lymphoma Near-Infrared Fluorescence Imaging with Indocyanine Green for Intraoperative Detection of Canine Urothelial Carcinoma Bladder tumor Canine Novel Immunotherapy for Cats with Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma Carcinoma (other) Feline Pilot Clinical Trial of Trametinib Treatment in Dogs with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) Canine Leukemia 3D-Printed Implant that Replaces Bone for Limb Sparing in Giant Breed Dogs with a Primary Bone Tumor Canine Osteosarcoma Minimally invasive sentinel lymph node mapping for canine apocrine gland anal sac adenocarcinoma (AGASACA) Canine Carcinoma (other) Combination of Lapatinib and Fractionated Radiation Therapy for Dogs with Transitional Cell Carcinoma Bladder tumor Canine Clinical Testing of Canine T cells Engineered to Target Osteosarcoma and Soft Tissue Sarcoma in Combination with Oral Immunotherapy Canine Soft Tissue Sarcoma Dual Energy CT to Assess Bone Mineral Density in Dogs with Osteosarcoma Undergoing StereotacticBody Radiation Therapy Canine Osteosarcoma CAR T cell Immunotherapy for Treatment of Metastatic Osteosarcoma in Dogs Canine Osteosarcoma One Cure for pets and people with cancer Funding for our clinical trials program comes from generous donors to our One Cure initiative. One Cure’s founding principle is that cancer affects all creatures, and that treatment breakthroughs come through collaboration between scientists and doctors who are working with both pets and people. One Cure’s goal is to raise awareness and monetary support for clinical trials and comparative oncology research. Your gift supports the search for better treatment options for pets and people with cancer. ",
