          "text_length": 6269,
          "text_excerpt": "Clinical Trials Skip to main content myLSU Apply Visit Give Search Menu Search LSU.edu Close Menu Veterinary Hospital myLSU Apply Visit Give Search Search LSU.edu Close Emergencies Pets Pets Anesthesiology Cardiology Community Practice (Primary Care) Counseling Services Dentistry and Oral Surgery Dermatology, Ear and Allergy Diagnostic Imaging Emergency and Critical Care Integrative Medicine Internal Medicine Neurology Oncology Ophthalmology Physical Rehabilitation Surgery Theriogenology (Reproduction) All Hospital Services Exotics & Wildlife Exotics & Wildlife Zoological Medicine (Exotics) Wildlife All Hospital Services Horses Horses Ambulatory Service Internal Medicine Integrative Medicine Ophthalmology Surgery Anesthesiology Dermatology Diagnostic Imaging Ophthalmology Theriogenology (Reproduction) All Hospital Services Food Animals (Livestock) Food Animals (Livestock) Food Animal All Hospital Services Owners Owners Information for Pet and Animal Owners Hospital Services Veterinarians Veterinarians Information for Veterinarians Hospital Services Close Menu Home Veterinary Medicine Veterinary Hospital Clinical Trials Clinical Trials Submitted protocols are reviewed by both the Clinical Protocol Review Committee (LSU SVM Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences) and LSU’s Institutional Animal and Care Use Committee (IACUC) with the intent to ensure that welfare and confidentiality matters are observed. These reviews are patterned on “Good Clinical Practices” for conduct of clinical trials in human medicine. FURTHER INFORMATION ON GOOD CLINICAL PRACTICES Call for healthy dogs taking antibiotics Overview Criteria Benefits Contact and Enroll What if adding dietary fiber to a dog’s diet can protect them from the negative effects of antibiotics? Have you ever wondered if there is a simple way to help protect a dog's gut health while taking antibiotics? We are studying whether adding a dietary fiber to a dog's diet and giving fecal transplantation can reduce some of the unwanted effects antibiotics may have on the intestinal microbiome and overall health. By participating, your dog can help advance veterinary research that may improve the way veterinarians support dogs during and after antibiotic treatment. We are looking for healthy dogs to take part in this study. Participation involves collecting stool, blood, and urine samples at scheduled visits so our team can monitor how the body and gut microbiome respond. In addition to contributing to important research, participants receive free bloodwork and urinalysis, approximately $50 worth of fiber supplement, and $100 compensation upon completion of the study. Every enrolled dog helps us learn more about how we can support canine health, bringing us one step closer to developing better strategies for dogs that need antibiotic treatment in the future. Dr. Jisoo Hong Small Animal Intern Resident buonolab@lsu.edu ENROLL NOW Call for healthy dogs for atopic dermatitis study Overview Criteria Contact Dermatology, Ear and Allergy Service Join the Journey: Help Advance Research for Healthier Dogs Worldwide! The association between chronic passive lead and cadmium exposure and atopic dermatitis in dogs – a preliminary study. Heavy metals are present throughout the ecosystem. Heavy metal exposure is associated with a wide variety of health effects in humans and nowadays has become an important health concern. In humans, allergies have been associated with heavy metal exposure. Canine and human allergies share many similarities; therefore, the goal of our study is to see if there is an association between heavy metals exposure and skin allergies in dogs. We are seeking healthy (non-allergic) dog participants. Sampling is not painful and does not require sedation. At the visit, swab samples will be taken from various areas on the body. As well as 2 teaspoons of blood. Thank you for helping advance research that will improve the lives of dogs worldwide! Healthy dogs with no signs of skin all",
