          "text_excerpt": "Oncology Clinical Trials | Research | Gary R. Carlson, MD, College of Veterinary Medicine | Oregon State University Skip to main content Gary R. Carlson, MD, College of Veterinary Medicine Research Search Search Open Search Page About Directory Dean's Message Administration Advisory Council Fact Sheet History Previous Deans Veterinary Reporting Form for Animals Injured by Trapping Devices Academics DVM Program Comparative Health Sciences Graduate Program One Health Undergraduate Certificate Fellowships, Residencies and Internships Teaching Hospital Diagnostic Laboratory Research Departments Department of Biomedical Sciences Department of Clinical Sciences Community Alumni Apparel Recruit at CCVM FURst Aid Animal Lover Education Series Giving High School Summer Veterinary Experience Pet Memorial Pet Day Tours Continuing Education Veterinary Pulse Newsletter Sign-up Alphabetized Research Lab List Arnold Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Beechler Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Bermudez Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Cebra Lab (Clinical Sciences) Chappell Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Curran Lab (Clinical Sciences) Danelishvili Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Dolan Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Estill Lab (Clinical Sciences) Hall Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Häse Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Jin Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Johns Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Jolles Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Kamm Lab (Clinical Sciences) Kent Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Leeper Lab (Clinical Sciences) Löhr Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Magnusson Lab (Biomedical Sciences) McKenzie Lab (Clinical Sciences) Medlock Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Moulton Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Pastey Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Ramsey Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Rockey Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Sanders Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Sarker Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Scollan Lab (Clinical Sciences) Semevolos Lab (Clinical Sciences) Shulzhenko Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Summers Lab (Clinical Sciences) Tornquist Lab (Biomedical Sciences) Warnock Lab (Clinical Sciences) Zellmer Lab (Clinical Sciences) Research Directory Student Research Opportunities Breadcrumb Gary R. Carlson, MD, College of Veterinary Medicine Research Oncology Clinical Trials Oncology Clinical Trials What are clinical trials? Clinical trials are a type of research. They are an important component of all specialties of medicine and help us as researchers and veterinarians to evaluate new types of diagnostics and treatments. Clinical trials are variable and may be designed to answer different questions including is a new treatment more effective, is a new diagnostic more specific, or does a new therapy combination have less side effects. Who can join clinical trials? The Lois Bates Acheson Veterinary Teaching Hospital has many clinical trials underway at any one time. It is possible that a pet may be eligible for more than one ongoing clinical trial. There are often initial tests such as a physical exam, blood work and/or imaging that are needed to ensure a patient is approved for enrollment in a trial. All clinical trials have a unique set of inclusion and exclusion criteria that help the research team enroll the correct patient population. Your pet’s oncology clinicians will discuss standard treatment and clinical trial treatment options with you. It is your decision as to whether or not you would like your pet to participate in a clinical trial. What are the benefits of participating in a clinical trial? There are several important benefits to participating in clinical trials. You and your pet will be personally involved in expanding the scientific knowledge of oncology in veterinary patients. Your pet may also receive novel diagnostics and/or treatments, and may receive these services at reduced or no cost. The potential benefits of a clinical trial are varied and would be discussed thoroughly prior to your pet’s enrollment. Contact information: Please do not hesitate to contact the teaching hospital and the oncology service for more information on clinical trials. ",
