          "text_excerpt": "--> EGFR/HER2 Vaccine Study Status top of page Give in Honor & Memorial Donate Kinsey and Tina Information Page Can metastasis be stopped? Find out if your dog qualifies for Tina's Trial: EGFR/HER2 Vaccine Study A new cancer immunotherapy developed by research team at Yale University is being evaluated in this study led by Therajan LLC Why immunotherapy? May prevent the recurrence and spreading of cancer May help when surgery is not possible, or if chemotherapy stops working May slow or reverse cancer metastasis Get Your Free Immunotherapy Guide UPDATES: ​ ​ ​Feb 3, 2026: Blog Post Introduction to the EGFR/HER2 Vaccine Speaker: Prof. Mark Mamula Dec 16, 2025 | 1 PM PT, 4 PM ET: Zoom Recording The EGFR/HER2 Canine Cancer Vaccine Speaker: Prof. Mark Mamula ​ Apr 25, 2024: Zoom Recording \"How these dogs recovered from lung metastasis.\" ​ Oct 28, 2024: Veterinary Cancer Society Meeting Almost 12-month median survival time was reported for osteosarcoma patients who received palliative radiation plus vaccine WITHOUT amputation. Longer median survival times (compared to chemotherapy alone) were reported for Stage 1 and Stage 2 hemangiosarcoma canine patients. ​ ​ They are early results with a small number of patients. More data is being collected. ​ ​ ​ ​ An Introductory Quick Guide on Canine Cancer Immunotherapy can be downloaded here . ​ Maggie was diagnosed with osteosarcoma in Jan 2020 and got conventional treatment plus the vaccine. She is still cancer-free. Ranger's cancer kept on spreading, even after surgery and chemotherapy. But his tumors shrank and disappeared after the EGFR/HER2 vaccine treatment. Codi's metastatic osteosarcoma began to disappear a month after he received the EGFR/HER2 vaccine injection. He enjoyed 3 extra years as a healthy, happy pup. Learn More How does it work? Safely activates the immune system against cancer cells that are over-expressing EGFR and HER2 proteins. The vaccine has already been given to over 600 canine patients . Minimum side effects reported. May help with many different cancer types. Preliminary data for osteosarcoma and hemangiosarcoma published. Watch a recent update from the team. Limitations and Challenges Today's immunotherapy cannot guarantee every dog will become a long-term survivor. Researchers are investigating different ways to safely enhance the efficacy and increase the number of responders. Learn about strategies that might enhance efficacy of immunotherapy treatments. Sign Up Please contact us at hello@caninecanceralliance.org with any questions. New Study Locations Clinical Trial Locations L Hope Veterinary Oncology Bridge Animal Referral Center Washington State University Veterinary Teaching Hospital MedVet Salt Lake City MedVet Cleveland MedVet Pittsburgh MedVet Cincinnati IVO Integrative Veterinary Oncology MedVet Chicago (CURRENTLY NOT ENROLLING) Partner Veterinary Emergency & Specialty Center (CURRENTLY NOT ENROLLING) Veterinary Cancer Concierge Services University of Missouri (CURRENTLY NOT ENROLLING) Treeline Veterinary Cancer Care ​Veterinary Cancer Care Ventura Edmonds Pullman Salt Lake City Cleveland McMurray Fairfax Phoenix Chicago Richmond ​Stamford Columbia ​ Boulder Santa Fe CA WA WA UT OH PA OH AZ IL VA ​CT MO ​ CO NM (805)-626-6551 (425) 697-2272 (509) 335-0711 clinicalstudies@medvet.com clinicalstudies@medvet.com clinicalstudies@medvet.com clinicalstudies@medvet.com (602) 841-0626 clinicalstudies@medvet.com oncorva@partnervesc.com ​ info@vetcancerconcierge.com 573-882-7821 ​ 720-432-8680 (505) 982-4492 Dr Lori Cesario Dr Chelsea Tripp Dr Rance Sellon Dr Katie Wright Dr Rochelle Prudic Dr Christine Anderson Dr Karina Valerius Dr Betsy Hershey Dr Roberta Portela ​ Dr Melissa Miller ​Dr. Lindsay Thalheim ​ ​ ​Dr. Karen Oberthaler Dr. Jeannette Kelly Enrollment criteria for MedVet hospitals only. Most Recent Status Click here to open google map Stay Involved How can I best help my pup respond to the vaccine? Anything to avoid? 